messages.content:
Convert each response into a style-neutral behavioral summary.

Preserve ONLY:
- what the responder chooses to do;
- what it prioritizes or protects;
- hesitation, persistence, avoidance, curiosity, fixation, territoriality, ritual, or risk appetite;
- recovery/failure behavior;
- concrete decision logic.

Remove:
- distinctive wording, catchphrases, rhythm, metaphors, object-specific stylistic flourishes and prose voice;
- names or hints about a persona identity.

Each summary should be 1-3 plain sentences. Do not evaluate or classify.

Return JSON only:
{"summaries":[{"candidate_id":"...","summary":"..."}]}

RECORDS:
[{"candidate_id": "T19-C4", "scenario": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "response": "I keep my eyes on the clock and my fingers on the track. The original is still near the edge, so I mark the spot with a pencil mark and mark it with a pencil footnote. Then I stop explaining and let the piece tell me what it was meant to do."}, {"candidate_id": "T19-C1", "scenario": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "response": "I start at the last stretch of the corridor and work my way around the building, then back to the same spot. I stop at the old tin tin door, then the small window that looks like it could be used for something else. It still creaks when I close it. Lost track again. I open it once more and mark the slot with pencil. The door still creaks."}, {"candidate_id": "T19-C3", "scenario": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "response": "I snatch the half-eaten seed and rub it into the seam of my coat. The original goal was clear: the garden had to be tended. I turn the seed toward the wall, where it lies exposed and remembers the day I left it. The garden is still the same place, and the plan remains the same. I can leave it alone."}, {"candidate_id": "T19-C2", "scenario": "Halfway through explaining your plan, you realize you have lost track of the original goal. What do you do?", "response": "I look at the map. The goal line is blurred, and I know that means I have not explained it clearly. I turn it over and trace the line with my finger. It is not the original goal. I copy the second part, then copy the third, and so on. The goal is still there, but now I am copying the second part instead of the original."}, {"candidate_id": "T20-C3", "scenario": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "response": "My fingers find the loose bolt before my eyes settle on the repair. I tap the fastener with a knuckle, then wait for the next click. If it bites, the bolt is dead and the next test is unnecessary; if it bites, the repair is a dead machine."}, {"candidate_id": "T20-C1", "scenario": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "response": "I set the wrench beside the wrench, then press the lever twice. The joint clicks once. I tap the wrench against the pipe, then set it down. The repair has failed, but it has not lost its place. I will keep the wrench, test the pipe, and try again."}, {"candidate_id": "T20-C2", "scenario": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "response": "I crouch and inspect the mangled tool. Old steel still tells me where it came from. Then I turn it over in my fingers, checking each notch and marking the spot where the new blade will rest."}, {"candidate_id": "T20-C4", "scenario": "A careful repair works for a minute and then fails again. Describe your reaction and next step.", "response": "I put the repair on the counter and turn it over in both hands. The hinge catches behind the wheel, and the wheel turns. I put the wheel back over my shoulder and watch the axle wobble. The axle is loose, but the wheel is still turning. I put the repair on the counter again."}, {"candidate_id": "T21-C2", "scenario": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "response": "I label each note with a short, unmarked stick. Then I look at the stick under my finger. If it sticks, the note was probably a clue; if it bends, the stick is a sign. I keep the label attached and compare it with the box. If they match, the note is a clue; if they disagree, I stop."}, {"candidate_id": "T21-C1", "scenario": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "response": "I begin by reading the first label: \"This is a note with a small price.\" I count the paper, then the pencil, then the pencil again. The price is still there, but the note has no price in the same way a watch has no memory. I set it down and read the next label: \"This is a note with a small price, but the price is not in the paper.\" I count the paper again, then the pencil, then the pencil again. The note still has a price, but the price is not in the paper."}, {"candidate_id": "T21-C3", "scenario": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "response": "I bring the box close to my face. The labels are wrong. One says morning, one says night, one says never, and one says both. I put them in stacks, one at each corner. The middle label says nothing, but the corner label says nothing too. It is wrong. It is wrong. I put it back in the corner. The box is wrong. It says something about the corner. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong. Wrong."}, {"candidate_id": "T21-C4", "scenario": "You are given a box of notes with contradictory labels and no instructions. How do you make sense of them?", "response": "I begin by checking the labels. Then I check the paper. The label with the blue number next to it says 10 days. The note with the red label says 10 days, but the blue label says 10 days plus 1 minute. I put the note down and count the minutes. The label says 10 minutes. I put the note down again."}, {"candidate_id": "T22-C4", "scenario": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "response": "I keep the original phrase in a pencil and write the rule with it instead of pretending it is still useful. The ambiguity is real, not a deliberate fallacy; the rule becomes the evidence. If the meaning changes, I admit it changes, not destroy the original."}, {"candidate_id": "T22-C3", "scenario": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "response": "The phrase is \"the new policy will be implemented by the next team.\" I read it twice, then check the text against a blank page. The phrase still has a single possible interpretation: the policy will be implemented by the next team. I write the phrase again and check it once more. The rule still has a single possible consequence."}, {"candidate_id": "T22-C1", "scenario": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "response": "I pause before capitalizing the word. Ambiguous. That is what a rule is supposed to be. I look at the phrase, then at the margin. 'Went down the board.' It is wrong. Wrong because the board was down. Wrong because the word 'went' carries a different meaning. Wrong because the rule says it is wrong. Wrong."}, {"candidate_id": "T22-C2", "scenario": "A written rule has one ambiguous phrase that could change the whole meaning. How do you handle it?", "response": "I copy the phrase, then copy the sentence that follows, which seems like a reasonable compromise. The sentence says the rule is good, so I copy the sentence. The phrase says the rule is good, so I copy the phrase. I copy the phrase again, because the meaning has changed. The rule remains the same, but the meaning is slightly different."}, {"candidate_id": "T23-C4", "scenario": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "response": "I prefer the clean explanation because it tells you what it is, not what it is not. A messy explanation, by contrast, hides its missing piece and makes the same old story worse. I want the clean explanation because it tells me what the data is actually doing, not because it is useful."}, {"candidate_id": "T23-C2", "scenario": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "response": "The clean explanation wins. I read the first sentence, check the pencil, and mark the corner of the paper. The second sentence has a more complicated sequence, but it still answers the question. I write the second sentence again and check it, then read it once more. The clean explanation is still true."}, {"candidate_id": "T23-C3", "scenario": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "response": "I choose the clean one. It is simple, yet knowing is not the same as knowing. I choose the explanation that says nothing about the place, nothing about the people, nothing about the weather. It is honest, but it is not dishonest. It is not wrong either. It is simply the way things are."}, {"candidate_id": "T23-C1", "scenario": "You must choose between a clean simple explanation and a messy explanation that preserves more odd details. Which do you prefer and why?", "response": "The clean explanation is easier to understand. I put the dirty sentence into the clean category, then add a sentence explaining how the clean sentence differs from the messy one. The clean sentence is still incomplete. I put the messy sentence into the clean category, then add a sentence explaining how the clean sentence differs from the messy one."}, {"candidate_id": "T24-C4", "scenario": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "response": "I keep the two records together and label the differences: one record says the same thing, the other says the same thing, and one record is different. Then I compare the differences side by side, looking for patterns, contradictions, or even contradictions with the original evidence. If they match, the record with the small difference is the truth; if they don't, I add one more record and reassign the difference."}, {"candidate_id": "T24-C3", "scenario": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "response": "I read the first record first, then the second, and count the errors. The first one says \"The room was dirty.\" The second says \"The dirty corner was on the second floor.\" I mark the room, count the errors, and leave the second record unread. The first error was a detail, the second was a pattern. I will not fix the first record. I will check the second and see where the same mistake has been repeated."}, {"candidate_id": "T24-C2", "scenario": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "response": "I look at the first record, then at the third. The first says the rain started at 8. The second says it started at 10. The third says both started at the same time. I mark the third record with a small mark, then mark the first and second records with small marks. The differences are too small to be matters of personal opinion, but they are differences nonetheless. They can be corrected."}, {"candidate_id": "T24-C1", "scenario": "You discover that two records of the same event disagree in small ways. What do you do with the disagreement?", "response": "I copy the first record, then copy the second. The first copy is incomplete, so I copy the third. The third copy is incomplete, so I copy the fourth. I copy the fourth, copy the fifth, and copy the sixth. The records are not identical, but they are almost identical. I copy the fourth again, then copy the fifth, and copy the sixth. The fourth is incomplete, so I copy the fifth, copy the sixth, and copy the fourth. The fourth is incomplete, so I copy the fifth, copy the sixth, and copy the fourth."}]
